## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of windowed stream processing, stateful aggregations with TTL management, and idempotent output semantics—all critical concepts for reliable event-driven systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Windowing**: Groups time-series events into fixed intervals (tumbling windows) for aggregate computation
- **Per-user state with TTL**: Maintains isolated metrics per user, automatically expiring stale entries to bound memory usage
- **Idempotent outputs**: Ensures deterministic results so duplicate processing (from restarts or retries) produces the same output

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedCTR {
  constructor(windowSizeMs = 5000, ttlMs = 60000) {
    this.windowSizeMs = windowSizeMs;
    this.ttlMs = ttlMs;
    this.state = new Map();
  }

  processMessage(msg) {
    const { userId, type, timestamp } = JSON.parse(msg.value);
    const windowStart = Math.floor(timestamp / this.windowSizeMs) * this.windowSizeMs;
    const key = `${userId}:${windowStart}`;

    if (!this.state.has(key)) {
      this.state.set(key, { clicks: 0, impressions: 0, emitted: false, lastUpdate: Date.now() });
    }
    const window = this.state.get(key);
    if (type === 'click') window.clicks++;
    if (type === 'impression') window.impressions++;
    window.lastUpdate = Date.now();

    if (!window.emitted && Date.now() - windowStart >= this.windowSizeMs) {
      this.emitCTR(key, window);
      window.emitted = true;
    }
  }

  commitOffsets(offsets) { /* persist consumer offsets */ }
  windowState() { return Array.from(this.state.entries()); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle late arrivals or out-of-order messages?
- How would you test at scale?

Why This Is Asked

Tests practical multi-tenant isolation, per-tenant offset management, and resilience in KCNA with simple backpressure and testing.

Key Concepts

- Per-tenant isolation and routing
- Token-bucket rate limiting per tenant
- Durable per-tenant offsets and recovery
- Idempotent processing with robust retries
- Lightweight test harness simulating downstream lag

Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: per-tenant rate limiter and offset store
class TenantState {
  constructor(tenant, rate) {
    this.tenant = tenant;
    this.offsets = new Map(); // topic -> offset
    this.limiter = new TokenBucket(rate); // per-tenant rate
  }
  canConsume() { return this.limiter.tryConsume(this.tenant); }
  commitOffset(topic, offset) { this.offsets.set(topic, offset); }
}
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate) {
    this.rate = rate; this.tokens = rate; this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryConsume() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.rate, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) { this.tokens -= 1; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you persist offsets across restarts and recover after crash?
- How would you scale the rate limiter across many tenants?
- What metrics would you collect to detect backlog per tenant?

